1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,879
In nineteen thirty-seven,
one army ordered a chocolate

2
00:00:03,879 --> 00:00:07,777
bar that tasted only a little
better than a boiled potato.

3
00:00:07,777 --> 00:00:08,510
On purpose.

4
00:00:09,210 --> 00:00:13,787
Another army lost more soldiers to hunger
and disease than to enemy bullets.

5
00:00:13,787 --> 00:00:17,160
So which country fed its
soldiers best in World War Two?

6
00:00:17,860 --> 00:00:21,005
Today we rank six armies,
from starving to spoiled.

7
00:00:21,005 --> 00:00:23,040
And every army gets three scores.

8
00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:25,823
What the ration promised on paper.

9
00:00:25,823 --> 00:00:27,233
How it actually tasted.

10
00:00:27,233 --> 00:00:30,480
And the big one: did the
food ever reach the soldier?

11
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:34,570
Because a perfect menu means
nothing if the truck never arrives.

12
00:00:35,270 --> 00:00:38,100
Number six: the Imperial Japanese Army.

13
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:43,555
By nineteen forty-two, Japan held a huge
empire across Asia and the Pacific.

14
00:00:43,555 --> 00:00:46,120
But it could not feed the men holding it.

15
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:49,469
On paper, the Japanese soldier ate simply.

16
00:00:49,469 --> 00:00:54,640
His ration was mostly rice, with dried
fish, pickled plums and powdered soy paste.

17
00:00:55,340 --> 00:00:59,309
It was light, so a man could carry
several days of food on his back.

18
00:00:59,309 --> 00:01:01,060
But the plan had a fatal flaw.

19
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:06,263
Japanese commanders often expected
troops to find food where they fought.

20
00:01:06,263 --> 00:01:11,260
So when American submarines sank the supply
ships, whole islands ran out of food.

21
00:01:11,960 --> 00:01:15,704
On Guadalcanal, Japanese soldiers
gave the island a new name.

22
00:01:15,704 --> 00:01:17,730
They called it Starvation Island.

23
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:23,100
On New Guinea, cut-off units survived on
grass, roots and anything they could catch.

24
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:27,358
Imagine a nineteen-year-old
private on a jungle island.

25
00:01:27,358 --> 00:01:28,781
His rice bag is empty.

26
00:01:28,781 --> 00:01:31,951
The next supply ship is
at the bottom of the sea.

27
00:01:31,951 --> 00:01:33,310
And nobody is coming.

28
00:01:34,010 --> 00:01:37,649
Japanese historian Yoshida
Yutaka estimates that about

29
00:01:37,649 --> 00:01:41,692
sixty percent of Japan's military
dead never died in battle.

30
00:01:41,692 --> 00:01:44,230
They died from starvation and disease.

31
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:46,406
So Japan finishes last.

32
00:01:46,406 --> 00:01:51,540
Not because the rice was bad, but because
for over a million men, it never came.

33
00:01:52,240 --> 00:01:55,683
But hunger was not the only way
to ruin a soldier's dinner.

34
00:01:55,683 --> 00:01:58,950
Some armies had food, and
their soldiers still hated it.

35
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:01,760
Number five: Italy.

36
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:05,940
Italian soldiers in North
Africa lived on a hard biscuit

37
00:02:05,940 --> 00:02:09,110
called the galletta, and
small tins of boiled beef.

38
00:02:09,810 --> 00:02:14,440
The tins were stamped with two letters, A
and M, for the Military Administration.

39
00:02:15,140 --> 00:02:17,393
But soldiers had their own meaning.

40
00:02:17,393 --> 00:02:20,547
A popular story says they
read it as Asino Morto.

41
00:02:20,547 --> 00:02:21,320
Dead donkey.

42
00:02:22,020 --> 00:02:25,916
And when German troops ate the
same tins, they had a joke too.

43
00:02:25,916 --> 00:02:26,607
Alter Mann.

44
00:02:26,607 --> 00:02:27,110
Old man.

45
00:02:27,810 --> 00:02:32,500
So in the desert heat, the same beef and
the same biscuits came back, day after day.

46
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:37,423
And the Italian army had far fewer
trucks than the British it was fighting.

47
00:02:37,423 --> 00:02:40,070
So food often moved slowly,
if it moved at all.

48
00:02:40,770 --> 00:02:43,869
But Italy had one comfort
that most armies did not.

49
00:02:43,869 --> 00:02:47,090
Its soldiers were entitled
to a daily ration of wine.

50
00:02:47,790 --> 00:02:50,268
That small glass lifts Italy above Japan.

51
00:02:50,268 --> 00:02:54,500
But a glass of wine was nothing
compared to what the next army poured.

52
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:57,690
Number four: the Soviet Red Army.

53
00:02:58,390 --> 00:03:00,923
Its ration was built on one thing.

54
00:03:00,923 --> 00:03:01,370
Bread.

55
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:05,544
From September nineteen
forty-one, a front-line soldier

56
00:03:05,544 --> 00:03:08,828
was promised eight hundred
grams of rye bread a day.

57
00:03:08,828 --> 00:03:10,330
In winter, nine hundred.

58
00:03:11,030 --> 00:03:14,614
That is almost a kilo of heavy
black bread, every single day.

59
00:03:14,614 --> 00:03:19,490
Bread came first because it was cheap,
filling, and could be baked almost anywhere.

60
00:03:20,190 --> 00:03:23,923
On the march, it became hard dried
crusts that lasted for weeks.

61
00:03:23,923 --> 00:03:28,240
A soldier soaked them in tea or soup,
or just chewed for a very long time.

62
00:03:28,940 --> 00:03:31,483
But the Red Army also had a famous extra.

63
00:03:31,483 --> 00:03:35,390
Front-line troops were issued one
hundred grams of vodka a day.

64
00:03:36,090 --> 00:03:39,710
But the biggest surprise in Soviet
mess tins came from America.

65
00:03:40,410 --> 00:03:42,849
Through Lend-Lease, the
United States shipped

66
00:03:42,849 --> 00:03:45,450
huge amounts of canned
meat to the Soviet Union.

67
00:03:46,150 --> 00:03:49,233
Soldiers gave the American
cans a sarcastic nickname.

68
00:03:49,233 --> 00:03:51,210
They called them the second front.

69
00:03:51,910 --> 00:03:54,805
Because for years, American
cans reached Russia

70
00:03:54,805 --> 00:03:57,700
long before American
soldiers landed in France.

71
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:00,367
So the Red Army sits in the middle.

72
00:04:00,367 --> 00:04:03,740
Plenty of bread, a little vodka,
and a lot of borrowed meat.

73
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:06,803
But its enemy had a
ration so precious that

74
00:04:06,803 --> 00:04:09,330
soldiers could be punished
just for eating it.

75
00:04:10,030 --> 00:04:12,960
Number three: the
Wehrmacht, the German army.

76
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:17,860
Every German soldier carried an emergency
ration called the iron portion.

77
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:22,150
Hard biscuits, a tin of meat,
dried vegetables, coffee and salt.

78
00:04:22,850 --> 00:04:25,530
He could only eat it when
his commander allowed it.

79
00:04:25,530 --> 00:04:27,790
Men who opened it early
could be locked up.

80
00:04:28,490 --> 00:04:32,270
Day to day, the army cooked hot
meals in mobile field kitchens.

81
00:04:32,270 --> 00:04:34,670
Soldiers nicknamed them goulash cannons.

82
00:04:35,370 --> 00:04:38,127
In the good years, this
system worked very well.

83
00:04:38,127 --> 00:04:42,320
But it depended on trucks, trains and
horses, and Russia broke all three.

84
00:04:43,020 --> 00:04:45,565
At Stalingrad, the
trapped Sixth Army ran so

85
00:04:45,565 --> 00:04:48,400
short of food that it
slaughtered its own horses.

86
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:53,195
For many German soldiers, Christmas
nineteen forty-two meant snow,

87
00:04:53,195 --> 00:04:56,360
frozen fingers and a thin
soup made from horsemeat.

88
00:04:57,060 --> 00:04:58,741
So Germany takes third place.

89
00:04:58,741 --> 00:05:01,930
A brilliant plan that collapsed
when the roads ran out.

90
00:05:02,630 --> 00:05:05,496
But the next army solved
the problem differently.

91
00:05:05,496 --> 00:05:08,420
It packed a whole day of
food into one wooden box.

92
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:11,540
Number two: the British Army.

93
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:14,873
Its secret weapon was
the Composite Ration.

94
00:05:14,873 --> 00:05:16,710
Soldiers just called it compo.

95
00:05:17,410 --> 00:05:19,650
One crate fed fourteen
men for a whole day.

96
00:05:20,350 --> 00:05:23,027
Inside were tins of steak
and kidney pudding,

97
00:05:23,027 --> 00:05:26,120
Scotch broth, bacon, vegetables
and a sweet pudding.

98
00:05:26,820 --> 00:05:29,540
There were biscuits, jam,
chocolate and cigarettes too.

99
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:33,033
But the most British thing
in the box was the tea.

100
00:05:33,033 --> 00:05:35,770
It came already mixed with
sugar and milk powder.

101
00:05:36,470 --> 00:05:40,280
So a soldier only needed hot water
to brew up, almost anywhere.

102
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:44,827
In the desert, in the jungle or
in a muddy Normandy hedgerow,

103
00:05:44,827 --> 00:05:48,170
a hot brew was the moment a
soldier felt human again.

104
00:05:48,870 --> 00:05:52,031
The crates came in different
menus marked with letters.

105
00:05:52,031 --> 00:05:54,790
But men often got the same
menu again and again.

106
00:05:55,490 --> 00:05:59,290
Still, Britain earns second place
for comfort, variety and that tea.

107
00:05:59,990 --> 00:06:02,791
But the winner had something
the British did not.

108
00:06:02,791 --> 00:06:05,820
Money, factories, and a very
strange idea of dessert.

109
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:08,860
Number one: the United States.

110
00:06:09,560 --> 00:06:12,174
Remember that chocolate
bar from the start?

111
00:06:12,174 --> 00:06:17,280
In nineteen thirty-seven, an army officer
named Paul Logan asked Hershey to make it.

112
00:06:17,980 --> 00:06:21,691
He wanted it to taste only a little
better than a boiled potato.

113
00:06:21,691 --> 00:06:25,750
Because if it tasted good, soldiers
would eat it before the emergency.

114
00:06:26,450 --> 00:06:31,150
So the D ration bar was hard, bitter and
so dense that men struggled to bite it.

115
00:06:31,850 --> 00:06:34,135
The daily rations were not loved either.

116
00:06:34,135 --> 00:06:37,220
Soldiers grumbled about the
same canned hash and stew.

117
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:39,619
So why is America number one?

118
00:06:39,619 --> 00:06:42,840
Because it could deliver food
almost anywhere on Earth.

119
00:06:43,540 --> 00:06:48,410
A small K ration box held a full meal,
with coffee, candy, gum and cigarettes.

120
00:06:49,110 --> 00:06:52,058
For small groups, there
was the ten-in-one ration.

121
00:06:52,058 --> 00:06:54,180
One box fed ten men for a whole day.

122
00:06:54,880 --> 00:06:57,921
Behind the lines, field
kitchens served hot meals,

123
00:06:57,921 --> 00:07:00,840
and on Thanksgiving, many
units even got turkey.

124
00:07:01,540 --> 00:07:03,010
But the real proof was dessert.

125
00:07:03,710 --> 00:07:07,420
In nineteen forty-five, the
United States Navy spent about

126
00:07:07,420 --> 00:07:10,810
one million dollars on a
concrete barge with one job.

127
00:07:11,510 --> 00:07:12,680
It made ice cream.

128
00:07:12,680 --> 00:07:15,150
About ten gallons every seven minutes.

129
00:07:15,850 --> 00:07:18,246
It was towed to the
Pacific to serve sailors

130
00:07:18,246 --> 00:07:20,750
on ships without their
own ice cream machines.

131
00:07:21,450 --> 00:07:24,771
So the country that made
chocolate taste bad on purpose

132
00:07:24,771 --> 00:07:27,790
also floated an ice cream
factory across an ocean.

133
00:07:28,490 --> 00:07:31,358
That is the gap between
starving and spoiled.

134
00:07:31,358 --> 00:07:33,588
On one island, a soldier ate grass.

135
00:07:33,588 --> 00:07:36,520
Across the same ocean,
a sailor ate ice cream.

136
00:07:37,220 --> 00:07:39,093
And the difference was not taste.

137
00:07:39,093 --> 00:07:41,080
It was ships, trucks and factories.

138
00:07:41,780 --> 00:07:43,685
So here is the final ranking.

139
00:07:43,685 --> 00:07:46,342
Japan, Italy, the Soviet Union, Germany,

140
00:07:46,342 --> 00:07:49,530
Britain, and at the very
top, the United States.

141
00:07:50,230 --> 00:07:52,219
But food was only half the deal.

142
00:07:52,219 --> 00:07:57,191
Next time, we rank how much every World
War Two army actually paid its soldiers.

143
00:07:57,191 --> 00:07:59,180
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
